# Simple OS – kernel, firmware and microcode from bookworm-backports (build: live-build config/archives;
# live/installed system: the same file as /etc/apt/preferences.d/simpleos-backports.pref).
# Why: the Bookworm kernel 6.1 does not know newer graphics chips (e.g. Intel Raptor Lake 0xA7AA–0xA7AD in the
# Core 5 210H, Core 7 240H …) -> i915 does not bind, the screen stays on the firmware framebuffer (800x600).
# Kernel 6.12 from backports knows them. The firmware must match (firmware-nonfree 2025: Intel graphics is then
# in firmware-intel-graphics), headers for DKMS (nvidia-driver, broadcom-sta-dkms) must match the kernel.
# NVIDIA 535 stays with Bookworm: it is current there via bookworm-security (535.309), backports lags behind
# (535.216) – and the GSP firmware must match the driver version exactly (so firmware-nvidia-gsp is excluded).
# Only the legacy driver 470 (Kepler) comes from backports (newer kernel patches). Everything else stays Bookworm.
# Updates: backports packages get updates from backports (ButAutomaticUpgrades); the source is also in the
# installed system (Calamares sources-final + simpleos-post-install).
# The GSP firmware belongs to the NVIDIA 535 driver (Bookworm, exactly the same version needed). MUST come before
# "firmware-*": apt takes the FIRST matching entry (otherwise 535.216 would come from backports and nvidia-driver
# 535.309 could no longer be installed)
Package: firmware-nvidia-gsp
Pin: release n=bookworm-backports
Pin-Priority: 100

Package: linux-image-* linux-headers-* linux-kbuild-* linux-compiler-* linux-base
Pin: release n=bookworm-backports
Pin-Priority: 990

Package: firmware-* intel-microcode amd64-microcode
Pin: release n=bookworm-backports
Pin-Priority: 990

Package: *nvidia-tesla-470*
Pin: release n=bookworm-backports
Pin-Priority: 990
